16
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.733
And now you are about to learn
how to retrieve data

17
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.733
based on their context or meaning.

18
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.300
The first step is to gather data.

19
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.833
This can be structured data like data
organizing tables or spreadsheet

20
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:05.500
with defined columns or unstructured data
such as audio and image data.

21
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.600
The next step involves passing the data
as input to an embedding model.

22
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.800
The output of an embedding
model is a vector.

23
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.500
At this point,
you can say that the initial data

24
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.000
has been vectorized, and

25
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.100
you're left
with the numerical representation

26
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.900
of the data that captures the context
and semantics of the data.

27
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.000
This is referred to as vector

28
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.633
embedding. In a high dimensional space,
referred to as a vector space,

29
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:32.633
you can compare the distance
between two embedding vectors or more

30
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.700
to get an indication
of how closely they are

31
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.700
similar in semantics or context.

32
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.800
So far you understand
vector search and information

33
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.333
retrieval technique
that uses numerical representation of data

34
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.233
known as vector embeddings
to search and retrieve information.

35
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.333
You also understand that

36
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:54.900
traditionally, information
retrieval relies on keyword matching,

37
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.100
which searches for direct matches
between query text

38
00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:00.333
and the text within the data set.

39
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:04.500
However, vector search makes use of this
embeddings to enable advanced

40
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.100
functionality such as semantic search,

41
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.100
which understands the context of the query.

42
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.333
Recommendation systems that predicts
user preference. And Retrieval

43
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:17.000
Augmented Generation,

44
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.733
or RAG, which provides
additional context for LLM inputs.

45
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.500
These capabilities make vector search a
powerful tool in various AI applications.

46
00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:30.800
Once data,
both structured and unstructured,

47
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.800
has been collected
and encoded into vector embeddings,

48
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:37.133
there is a requirement
to store the vectorized data

49
00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:41.400
into a specialized data store,
referred to as a vector database.

50
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.933
Within a vector database,

51
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:46.700
to ensure efficient retrieval of vector
data based on vector search queries,

52
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
it is best practice
to index the vector data.

53
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.800
A vector
search index is a specialized structure

54
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.433
that optimizes the storage
and retrieval of vector embeddings,

55
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.500
allowing for efficient
similarity searches.

56
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.833
So when a vector search operation
is performed, the index facilitates

57
00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:07.333
the efficient matching of the query vector
against the data set,

58
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:11.100
reducing the time needed
to find the most similar vectors.

59
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.000
And that takes you down

60
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.000
the road of search, specifically vector
search in retrieval

61
00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.500
augmented generation system.

62
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.800
Retrieval augmented generation
or RAG, is a system design pattern

63
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.333
that leverages information retrieval
techniques, including vector search

64
00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:33.833
and foundation models, to provide accurate
and relevant response to user queries.

65
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.133
RAG achieved this by retrieving
semantically similar data to supplement

66
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:42.600
user queries with additional context, and
then combining the retrieved information

67
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.533
with the original query
as input into large language models.

68
00:03:46.933 --> 00:03:50.400
For example, a typical process
using a chat interface

69
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.733
would be you enter your chat
and then you get a response from the LLM.

70
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.533
This is not the ideal process,
as this doesn't use

71
00:03:58.533 --> 00:04:02.900
any relevant data. The ideal process
would be, with the input to the LLM,

72
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.900
then you add in relevant domain
specific data, and the large language

73
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.233
model can provide relevant
and context-aware response to your query.

74
00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:16.933
Now that you have an understanding of RAG,
let's get an overview of the key

75
00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:20.700
benefits of RAG design pattern
for LLM applications.

76
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.433
Building AI application that leverages
RAG system design pattern provides

77
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:30.400
a number of benefits, such
as grounding the LLM response in relevant

78
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.500
and up-to-date information,
which will reduce the chances

79
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.900
of hallucinations
when the LLM essentially provides

80
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:41.033
wrong information
or irrelevant information. With retrieval

81
00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:42.233
augmented generation,

82
00:04:42.233 --> 00:04:45.400
you also have the benefit
of reducing the amount of information

83
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.200
that is passed as input into the LLM.

84
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.200
This can reduce the context
you pass into the context window. With RAG,

85
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.100
you also removed the need for fine
tuning LLMs in some scenario,

86
00:04:57.433 --> 00:05:00.800
but more specifically using retrieval
augmented generation,

87
